  "worked_solution": "Let $r$ be the radius of the sphere, $V$ the volume of the sphere, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{(\\frac{A}{4\\pi })}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =12\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & ={(12\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2} \\\\ A & =144\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =144\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
